From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 02:12:26 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 082/342] bfs: grow volumes in place; clear the new bitmap in
 pieces

Initialize takes "grow_limit <bytes>" (k/m/g suffixes): the log goes
behind a block bitmap large enough for a volume of that size and gets
that volume's log size, the blocks in between reserved like the log.
Growing such a volume up to the limit then moves nothing: no inode walk,
no log move, only the new bitmap blocks and the superblock. That makes
it safe for a mounted boot volume (a first-boot grow on the ODROID-C2).
With 2 KB blocks the limit can be up to about 384 GB, which still fits
the first allocation group with the default group size; 256 GB costs
24 MB of a 300 MB image.

Growing also cleared all new bitmap blocks in one transaction, which
failed for more than the log could hold: 300 MB to 256 GB adds 8192
blocks for a 4096 block log ("transaction too large"). They are now
cleared in transactions of half the log; the superblock keeps the old
size until all are done.

Tested in bfs_shell: 300 MB to 8, 64 and 256 GB in place (0.01-0.14 s),
then to 300 GB the old way, checkfs clean and file contents intact after
each step; a volume without a limit grows as before.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
